course: dictionary
lesson: 605
title: Awe: wonder mixed with respect
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Awe is a strong feeling of wonder and respect, sometimes with a little fear. You feel it when you see something huge, beautiful or powerful, like a mountain or the night sky. As a verb, «The mountains awed us» means the mountains filled us with awe.
explain: Context gives you clues. Look at «stand in awe» and «awe-inspiring»: the words around awe tell you it is a big, serious feeling, not a small one. Ask yourself: is this person happy, scared or amazed? If the scene is huge and beautiful, the word is probably awe.
explain: In The Wind in the Willows, the Mole feels «a great Awe» when he meets Pan. The book is careful to say it is not panic. Panic is sudden, wild fear, but awe is deep wonder and peace, with a little trembling.
fact: Awe is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say «a feeling of awe», not «an awe».
fact: Awe can also be a verb: awe, awes, awed, awing. «The mountains awed us» means they filled us with wonder.
fact: «Awe-inspiring» means so impressive that it fills you with awe.
fact: «Awestruck» means full of awe, and «in awe of» means feeling great wonder and respect for someone or something.
fact: Awe is not panic: panic is sudden fear, but awe is deep wonder, peace and respect.
exercise: «The mountains awed us.» What does awed mean here? | *Filled us with wonder and respect | Made us hungry | Made us bored and sleepy | Made us run away in panic
exercise: «The sunset was awe-inspiring.» What does awe-inspiring mean? | *So impressive that it fills you with wonder | Very dull and grey | Very loud and noisy | Very small and quiet
exercise: In The Wind in the Willows, Mole feels «a great Awe». What is the difference between awe and panic? | Awe is sleepiness; panic is hunger | Awe is anger; panic is joy | *Awe is deep wonder and peace; panic is sudden wild fear | Awe and panic mean exactly the same thing
test: «Everyone stood in awe as the rocket lifted off.» What does «stand in awe» mean? | Run home quickly | Fall asleep in the grass | *Stand still, filled with wonder and respect | Stand up and cheer loudly
test: Which word in «The night sky awes the children every summer» is the verb? | *awes | children | night | sky
exam: In The Wind in the Willows, when Mole meets the Piper, which feeling does the book say he has? | *Deep wonder and peace | Panic | Hunger | Boredom
